{"judgments": [
 {"clause_id": "c046", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The first sentence requires obligations rated in the highest short-term category \"by one of two nationally recognized statistical reporting organizations,\" while the next sentence requires the highest rating from \"at least one\" NRSRO — one or two ratings cannot both be the test. The provision also permits unrated obligations \"determined to be of comparable quality by Wells Fargo\" with no stated criteria.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c047", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Investment Manager is \"authorized to invest assets of the Fund in\" a closed list of nine categories with no catch-all, and the categories themselves (\"asset-backed securities,\" \"corporate notes, bonds, debentures,\" \"commercial paper and participations\") are undefined. Whether structured or hybrid instruments fall inside those categories, or are excluded because unlisted, is the question the list raises.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c048", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The 5% cap is by \"obligations of any one issuer\" measured only \"at the time of purchase,\" but the provision does not say who the issuer is for asset-backed, pass-through or repurchase-agreement exposures, nor whether the limit must be restored after it is breached by later events. The term \"issuer\" carries more than one reasonable meaning for the instruments the list authorizes.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c049", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The 35% cap applies to \"variable rate securities,\" but the Investment Objectives provision distinguishes \"fixed, variable, or floating\" rate securities, leaving it open whether floating rate instruments count against the cap. The distinction is nowhere defined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c050", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Illiquid securities as that term is defined in the SEC's Rule 2a-7\" imports a definition from a rule applicable to registered money market funds that the Fund does not follow, and Rule 2a-7's own illiquidity limit differs from the 15% stated here. What the incorporated definition covers is uncertain.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The logic is inverted: private placements are \"generally considered liquid unless at least two broker/dealers provide an active secondary market,\" so on its face the existence of active market makers makes a security illiquid. The mechanical error reverses the intended test.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.45}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The 20% overnight floor states no measuring date — unlike the 5% and 35% limits, which are expressly tested \"at the time of purchase\" — so whether it is a continuous requirement or a purchase-time test is unresolved. Nor is \"assets scheduled to mature overnight\" defined for repurchase agreements or demand instruments.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
